  <p>
    Gokarna has a reputation as the laid-back, less commercial answer to Goa — and for backpackers,
    that reputation holds up at the wallet too. It's entirely possible to spend a comfortable week
    here on a genuine backpacker budget, as long as you know roughly what things cost and where the
    money actually goes.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the money goes</h2>
  <p>
    The three real costs when backpacking Gokarna are your bed, your food, and getting around — in
    that order of impact on your daily budget.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Bed:</strong> a dorm bed in a hostel on or near Kudle hilltop typically starts around
      ₹{SITE.dormPriceFrom}/night. A private double room costs more, but splitting one between two
      people often lands close to a dorm bed each.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Food:</strong> Gokarna's beach shacks and hilltop cafes serve full meals — dosas,
      thalis, seafood curries — for well under what you'd pay in Goa's touristy strips. A cafe on
      the hostel property itself also means you're not walking into town for every meal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Getting around:</strong> Gokarna town, Kudle Beach, and Om Beach are all walkable or a
      short auto-rickshaw ride apart. Renting a bike is worth it only if you're planning day trips
      further out (Yana Caves, Mirjan Fort).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Time it right</h2>
  <p>
    October to March is the sweet spot — pleasant temperatures, low humidity, and the beach trek
    trails aren't slippery from monsoon rain. Prices also tend to soften outside the
    December–January peak, so shoulder months (October, November, February, March) are where budget
    backpackers get the best value.
  </p>

  <h2>The five-beach walk costs nothing</h2>
  <p>
    One of the best things to do in Gokarna is entirely free: the coastal walk from Gokarna Main
    Beach through Kudle, Om, Half Moon, and Paradise Beach. It's about 6km and 3–4 hours at an easy
    pace, and it's the single best way to see the coast without paying for a boat or a tour.
  </p>

  <h2>Where a hilltop hostel actually saves you money</h2>
  <p>
    Staying somewhere like Aangan, on Kudle hilltop between the temple town and the beach, means
    you're already positioned on the walking route most backpackers take anyway — no auto-rickshaw
    fares just to start your day. A hostel with a cafe on-site also means fewer temptation-priced
    tourist-strip meals, and a common area where you'll likely split a taxi or a bike rental with
    other travelers heading the same direction.
  </p>

